# Evo2-GO Remodel

This is the cleaned-up working tree for the Evo2-to-Gene-Ontology project.
The immediate goal is not to rewrite the science pipeline. It is to move the
stable parts into a documented, installable package with command-line entry
points that are easy to run and test.

## Current Scope

The migrated command surface now covers database preparation through embedding
dataset assembly:

- Gene2GO object model and parsing helpers.
- Representative CDS mapping.
- Per-taxon representative CDS mapping driver.
- CDS length filtering for the Evo2 context window.
- Evo2 embedding extraction into a shared embedding root.
- Embedding dataset validation.
- GO term counting from selected trainable genes.
- True-path GO label expansion.
- GO vocabulary and sparse adjacency construction.
- Rerunnable CPU-side training input preparation from an existing embedding
  root.
- Taxon-holdout split manifest generation.

Model training code and architecture experiments will move later, after the
database and embedding layers have a stable command surface.

## Install

From this directory:

```bash
python3 -m pip install -e .
```

If you need ontology adjacency or true-path label commands:

```bash
python3 -m pip install -e '.[ontology]'
```

If you need embedding extraction or validation:

```bash
python3 -m pip install -e '.[embeddings]'
```

The current LLNL environment may already provide these dependencies. The
editable install is only for command discovery and package imports.

## Command Pattern

All migrated commands are available through one entry point:

```bash
evo2-go --help
evo2-go <command> --help
```

During development, without installing:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m evo2_go.cli --help
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m evo2_go.cli go-term-counts --help
```

The first one-command database workflow is:

```bash
python3 build_database.py \
  --config configs/build.example.toml \
  --project-root . \
  --dry-run
```

Use `--dry-run` first. The workflow prints each underlying Python command
without writing files. Remove `--dry-run` when the paths and scientific choices
are correct.

After embeddings exist, rerun labels, adjacency, label cache, and split
preparation separately from database assembly:

```bash
python3 prepare_training_inputs.py \
  --config configs/training_inputs.example.toml \
  --project-root . \
  --dry-run
```

This workflow writes `training_inputs_manifest.tsv` and
`training_inputs_steps.tsv` under the build directory.

Embedding jobs write into one shared root:

```text
<embedding_root>/
  embedding_dataset_manifest.tsv
  embedding_metadata.tsv.gz
  tax_<tax_id>/
    embedding_metadata.tsv.gz
    embedding_shards/
```

Run repeated embedding jobs against the same `embedding_root`. Completed taxa
and completed batch shards are skipped by default, and the root metadata is
rebuilt from every complete taxon in that shared root.

Embedding extraction expects a local Evo2 checkpoint by default:

```text
evo2_model/evo2_7b_base.pt
```

Copy or symlink that checkpoint into this tree before launching a real
embedding job, or set `[embeddings].model_path` in the build config to an
explicit site-local path.

## Stable Data Contracts

Training examples are keyed by:

```text
tax_id, GeneID, cds_id
```

Do not key examples by bare `GeneID`; GeneIDs can collide across taxa.

Gene2GO annotations are GeneID-level labels. They are not transcript, protein,
or isoform-specific. The current sequence policy is:

```text
(tax_id, GeneID) -> longest representative CDS -> Evo2 embedding -> GO labels
```

True-path labels expand explicit positive labels to `is_a` and `part_of`
ancestors from `go-basic.obo`.

